The author's dead.  Not in a way that we can grieve and send 
flowers to; as far as I know, the members of what we once called
the DevTeam are alive, if disbanded.  But as a corporate entity, as
a publishing, productive group of programmers, as custodian of the
product we call NetHack -- it's not just dead, it's a distant
memory.  May its memory be eternal.

Integrity is important, and I am a big believer in doing what I 
promise to do, and I don't like the idea of stealing something 
which belongs to anyone else.  I don't want the next DevTeam, the
group which has possibly started already (the Nicehack -- Nitro+
AceHack group which has this month released a NetHack 4.2) to 
become the next DevTeam through theft or piracy, and I don't think
they have done.  In my mind, it wasn't taking; it was inheiritance.
The pro forma challenge that was explicitly stated with the mass 
releases on April 1st gave the defunct DevTeam the perfect chance
to assert its continued existence -- even a partial bugfix 3.4.4
release would have been enough to quell the move to succeed them.

There was no release; there was no DevTeam.  There is now.  
Maybe; maybe it's just the start of one.  But it's what we've got.

